\": null, \"latency_us\": null}, {\"M\": 240, \"uuid\": \"e7c939ae\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null, \"latency_us\": null}], \"geomean_speedup\": null, \"decision\": \"reject-invalid-fix-next\", \"decision_reason\": \"Split-K structure is sound but invalid: the autotuner re-launches the atomic kernel during benchmarking with no reset_to_zero, overflowing C32 -> non-finite. Fix in c003 by adding reset_to_zero=['C32'] so every benchmarked launch (and the reset before the real launch) starts from a zeroed buffer. Does not falsify H3 (occupancy hypothesis still untested).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 2, \"skill_usage\": \"none (sm_80 outside KernelWiki scope)\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c003\", \"parent_id\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"751382379b8e95affe8a4999e329113c09a8d0195dd5ccc3f322fd1e666c2ed4\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17T10:50:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fixing the autotune reset (reset_to_zero=['C32']) makes the atomic Split-K valid, and the raised block count saturates HBM to beat cuBLAS on skinny M (tests H3).\", \"change_from_parent\": \"single change: added reset_to_zero=['C32'] to @triton.autotune so each benchmarked launch starts from a zeroed fp32 buffer; kernel/reduction otherwise identical to c002\", \"config\": {\"BLOCK_M\": \"autotuned{16,64,128,256}\", \"BLOCK_N\": \"autotuned{32,64,128}\", \"BLOCK_K\": 64, \"SPLIT_K\": \"autotuned{2,4,8,16}\", \"num_warps\": \"4|8\", \"num_stages\": 3, \"GROUP_SIZE_M\": null, \"reduction\": \"atomic\", \"adaptive\": true}, \"validation\": {\"static_checklist_passed\": true, \"all_workloads_correct\": true, \"tolerance_notes\": \"VALID 5/5 passed. fp32 atomic reduction stays within atol=rtol=0.01 matched_ratio=0.99 on all M. Numerics fine; performance is the problem.\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"M\": 4, \"uuid\": \"f439da26\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.44166492835552923, \"latency_us\": 98.6521614342928}, {\"M\": 48, \"uuid\": \"59ca23f5\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.404858662065879, \"latency_us\": 115.91680087149143}, {\"M\": 64, \"uuid\": \"67d4c8f3\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.3796012213133505, \"latency_us\": 120.17664283514023}, {\"M\": 128, \"uuid\": \"29ebd771\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.3325663126903508, \"latency_us\": 151.30624294281006}, {\"M\": 240, \"uuid\": \"e7c939ae\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.35572191564898725, \"latency_us\": 195.60448244214057}], \"geomean_speedup\": 0.38101543178172426, \"decision\": \"reject-revert-to-c001\", \"decision_reason\": \"Split-K via atomics is VALID but SLOWER than c001 (0.38x vs 0.56x) on every M. The atomic-add contention into the fp32 buffer + the full-C32 torch.zeros memset + the separate fp32->fp16 cast pass cost more than the occupancy gained. Partially informs H3: raw block count alone does not help if the reduction path adds traffic/contention. Next (c004): raise occupancy WITHOUT atomics/extra passes — single-M-tile GEMM (B read once), small BLOCK_N (~128 blocks), deterministic fp16 store, no split-K. c001 remains best valid baseline (0.56x).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 3, \"skill_usage\": \"none (sm_80 outside KernelWiki scope)\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c004\", \"parent_id\": \"c001\", \"source_sha256\": \"f2e50f5881c3f7b7c5482fcd84e6dec590070e25899798931bdc410b64c230a6\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17T10:55:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"A wider plain-GEMM autotune space (small BLOCK_N for ~128 blocks, deeper num_stages, L2 grouping) raises occupancy on skinny M and beats c001 without split-K's reduction overhead.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"reverted split-K/atomics back to deterministic plain fp16-store GEMM (like c001) but broadened autotune: added BLOCK_N=32, num_stages=5, GROUP_SIZE_M grouping, and larger BLOCK_N/BLOCK_K/MMA options; key=['M']\", \"config\": {\"BLOCK_M\": \"autotuned{16,32,64,128}\", \"BLOCK_N\": \"autotuned{32,64,128,256}\", \"BLOCK_K\": \"autotuned{32,64}\", \"SPLIT_K\": 1, \"num_warps\": \"4|8\", \"num_stages\": \"3|4|5\", \"GROUP_SIZE_M\": 8, \"reduction\": \"none\", \"adaptive\": false}, \"validation\": {\"static_checklist_passed\": true, \"all_workloads_correct\": true, \"tolerance_notes\": \"VALID 5/5. Deterministic fp16 store, identical to c001 numerics class. M=128 bit-exact (rel=0).\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"M\": 4, \"uuid\": \"f439da26\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.58, \"latency_us\": 76.8}, {\"M\": 48, \"uuid\": \"59ca23f5\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.53, \"latency_us\": 83.7}, {\"M\": 64, \"uuid\": \"67d4c8f3\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.54, \"latency_us\": 84.6}, {\"M\": 128, \"uuid\": \"29ebd771\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.54, \"latency_us\": 95.2}, {\"M\": 240, \"uuid\": \"e7c939ae\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.59, \"latency_us\": 118.7}], \"geomean_speedup\": 0.56, \"decision\": \"neutral-plateau\", \"decision_reason\": \"Geomean 0.56x ties c001; wider N-tiling/stages did not break the plateau. Key insight: for M>BLOCK_M, N-tiling occupancy re-reads B (num_pid_m>1) doubling traffic, and single-M-tile N-tiling caps at num_pid_n(<=128) blocks -> ~436 GB/s vs cuBLAS ~762 GB/s. Split-K is the ONLY way to raise occupancy while reading B exactly once; c003 tested it but atomic contention confounded H3. Next c005: deterministic two-pass split-K (partials to [SPLIT_K,M,N] fp32, separate reduce), M-adaptive SPLIT_K, no atomics -> clean test of the occupancy hypothesis. c001/c004 remain best valid baseline (0.56x).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 4, \"skill_usage\": \"none (sm_80 outside KernelWiki scope)\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c005\", \"parent_id\": \"c004\", \"source_sha256\": \"3b14609aefc5e162445367089d9a74ebc53f52ee69550e2a5261c7aed2b35a40\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17T11:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"B-read efficiency (not occupancy) is the bottleneck; loading B as coalesced [BLOCK_N,BLOCK_K] with wide BLOCK_K (128/256) and in-register transpose improves DRAM burst utilization and beats c004.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"load B as [BLOCK_N,BLOCK_K] (k contiguous inner) + tl.trans before dot; widened BLOCK_K to 128/256; otherwise same deterministic no-split-K path; key=['M']\", \"config\": {\"BLOCK_M\": \"autotuned{16,32,64,128,256}\", \"BLOCK_N\": \"autotuned{64,128,256}\", \"BLOCK_K\": \"autotuned{64,128,256}\", \"SPLIT_K\": 1, \"num_warps\": \"4|8\", \"num_stages\": 3, \"GROUP_SIZE_M\": 8, \"reduction\": \"none\", \"adaptive\": false}, \"validation\": {\"static_checklist_passed\": true, \"all_workloads_correct\": true, \"tolerance_notes\": \"VALID 5/5. Deterministic; M=128 bit-exact (rel=0).\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"M\": 4, \"uuid\": \"f439da26\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.57, \"latency_us\": 80.5}, {\"M\": 48, \"uuid\": \"59ca23f5\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.54, \"latency_us\": 83.0}, {\"M\": 64, \"uuid\": \"67d4c8f3\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.55, \"latency_us\": 84.9}, {\"M\": 128, \"uuid\": \"29ebd771\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.57, \"latency_us\": 89.4}, {\"M\": 240, \"uuid\": \"e7c939ae\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.62, \"latency_us\": 113.0}], \"geomean_speedup\": 0.57, \"decision\": \"marginal-adopt-best\", \"decision_reason\": \"0.57x, a hair above c001/c004 (0.56x) and now the best valid baseline. Wider BLOCK_K/coalesced-B gave only ~1% => Triton layout optimizer already coalesced c004's load; loads are not the ceiling. Analysis: small M (4-64) is HBM-bandwidth bound and we hit only ~55% of cuBLAS bandwidth, matching the fraction of active SMs (64 n-blocks / 108 SMs) => small-M is occupancy-limited. c003 split-K failed because SPLIT_K 8-16 shortened K-loops to 4-8 iters (no cp.async steady state). Untested: modest SPLIT_K=2-4 (>=16-iter loops) to raise blocks past 108 while keeping pipelining. Next c006 tests this decisive occupancy experiment.\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 5, \"skill_usage\": \"none (sm_80 outside KernelWiki scope)\"}\n","numLines":6,"startLine":1,"totalLines":6}}}
