5.0's autotuner — it re-launches each config during benchmarking; with `tl.atomic_add` and no `reset_to_zero`, C32 accumulated across hundreds of launches → fp16 overflow → non-finite. Confirmed the GEMM math was correct.\n- **c003**: atomic split-K is numerically fine but the atomic contention + full fp32 memset + separate cast pass, plus over-splitting (SPLIT_K 8–16 → K-loops of only 4–8 iters, no cp.async steady state), make it net-negative.\n- **c004/c005**: plain path plateaus at ~0.56–0.57× ≈ 55% of cuBLAS bandwidth, matching the fraction of active SMs — the small-M cases are **occupancy-limited**, and loads are already coalesced (wide BLOCK_K gave only ~1%).\n\n## Current state\n- `solution/solution.py` = **c005** (hash `3b14609a…`, matches recorded evidence) — the best valid candidate.\n- Cumulative evaluations: **5**. All prior records untouched; all lines valid JSON.\n\n## Not converged — next step is justified\nThe decisive untested experiment is **modest SPLIT_K=2–4 with a deterministic two-pass reduction** (K-loops stay ≥16 iters to preserve cp.async pipelining; no atomic contention), which cleanly tests whether added occupancy while reading B exactly once beats the plateau. Because a concrete, justified `c006` remains, I did **not** write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`.\n\nStopping here per the one-candidate-per-turn directive (no `final` run performed).","ttft_ms":4746,"ttft_stream_ms":4745,"time_to_request_ms":113,"type":"result","duration_ms":2815443,"uuid":"e6383d07-88ad-4f91-9477-4dd779acf182"}
